# Fabric access widener — the Fabric-side twin of META-INF/accesstransformer_mojmap.cfg.
#
# ⚠️ THIS IS A TEMPLATE, NOT A WIDENER. It carries NO header line and it is never shipped: each
# Fabric node's buildscript expands it through `generateAccessWidener` (build-logic/AccessWidener.kt)
# into build/generated/accessWidener/alexsmobs.accesswidener, which is what loom validates, remaps
# and puts in the jar. Editing the generated copy accomplishes nothing.
#
# WHY a template. An AT entry naming an absent member is a silent no-op, so one .cfg can list both
# eras of a member that moved. An AW entry naming an absent member is a HARD ERROR — loom's
# `validateAccessWidener` refuses the build — and this file has to serve seventeen Fabric nodes from
# MC 1.20.1 to 26.2, across which the header namespace changes once and eight entries change
# descriptor, package or type. Hand-maintaining six near-identical files was the alternative.
#
# ⚠️ WHY IT IS NOT UNDER src/. Stonecutter DOES register .accesswidener as a `#`-comment file type
# and preprocesses the whole source tree, so a template kept there dies at stonecutterPrepare with
# "Extraneous input '{'" — the directives below are not its syntax. Native Stonecutter gating is not
# the answer either: its inactive arms come back as `#`-prefixed lines, and a widener's header must
# be the file's literal FIRST line, so the one thing that most needs gating is the one thing it
# cannot gate. Hence a plain root-level directory that Stonecutter never walks.
#
# DIRECTIVES (see AccessWidener.kt for the grammar):
#   #? <expr>     gates the next entry line
#   #?{ <expr>    opens a gated block …
#   #?}           … and closes it. Blocks do not nest, same house rule as the //? source gates.
# `<expr>` is the tree's ordinary version-predicate grammar; atoms are evaluated by Stonecutter
# itself, so `26` outranking `1.21.11` is decided in exactly one place. The generator emits the
# header: "official" on the unobfuscated 26.x nodes (loom-no-remap has no mappings tree and
# validates against the game's own names), "named" below, where classic loom remaps named →
# intermediary on the way into the jar and rejects an "official" header.
#
# TWO RULES WHEN EDITING:
#  1. A FIELD entry must carry the field's DESCRIPTOR, which an AT does not have. Read it off the
#     unpatched jar rather than guessing:
#       javap -p -s -cp <mc-merged.jar> net.minecraft.world.entity.Mob | grep -A1 ' navigation;'
#  2. Pre-flight EVERY version before touching a node:
#       scripts/aw_check.py accesswidener/alexsmobs.accesswidener 1.20.1 1.20.4 … 26.2
#     It understands these directives and reports the descriptor it DID find, so a moved member
#     gets a new gated arm rather than a deletion. `./gradlew :<node>:validateAccessWidener` is the
#     authority, but only answers one node at a time and only after a full loom setup.

accessible	field	net/minecraft/client/renderer/entity/LivingEntityRenderer	model	Lnet/minecraft/client/model/EntityModel;
accessible	field	net/minecraft/client/renderer/LevelRenderer	viewArea	Lnet/minecraft/client/renderer/ViewArea;
